      {
        "id": 139524,
        "name": "MigrantP",
        "username": "MigrantP",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/migrantp/{size}/1921_2.png",
        "created_at": "2015-04-15T20:43:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"AlexxKay\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/a/59ef9b/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> AlexxKay:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<blockquote>\nIf a card is damaging the X lowest HP targets, and in the middle of doing that a new lowest HP target appears, the targeting does not change. The originally chosen X targets are damaged. If one disappears in the middle, the damage fizzles and does not re-target.\n</blockquote>\nI would suggest rephrasing \"the damage fizzles\" to \"the damage to the missing target fizzles\".&nbsp; We're talking about (often) multiple targets here, and any that *don't* disappear do still take damage, right?&nbsp;\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Yes, I can tweak the wording there.</p>\n<blockquote></blockquote>\n<blockquote>1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ongoing effects on the card become applicable.2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Cards that react to other cards entering play now act.3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; One-time effects on the card happen.\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote>\nThat strikes me as needlessly complex.&nbsp; It would be much simpler to just make step 1 be \"Text on the card takes effect.\" and have no step 3.&nbsp; While one can easily construct an edge case where this makes a different outcome, I don't see any cases where it would cause an outcome that seems (to my intuition) <strong>wrong</strong> enough to make the added complexity worthwhile.&nbsp;\n<p>It's complex, but has to be that way or some weird things happen. It may not happen in tabletop play which is fine. We've learned that in the video game, if something weird <strong>can</strong> happen, it&nbsp;<strong>will</strong> happen to someone.</p>\n<blockquote></blockquote>\n<blockquote>Expatriette’s Ammo cards can be played without a gun in play. If that happens, the Ammo card is destroyed.\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote>\nI thought it went directly to the Trash, having never really entered play, and thus not triggering either \"card enters play\" or \"card was destroyed\" effects.&nbsp; Has this changed?&nbsp;\n<p>I don't know if it has changed, I just know what it is.</p>\n<blockquote></blockquote>\n<blockquote>If an ammo card is next to a gun card and the gun card is destroyed, the ammo card remains in play.\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote>\nUh, why?&nbsp; Expatriette has enough trouble getting ammo cards out, so why make it so easy for one to end up in limbo?&nbsp; She has many means of getting ammo out of her trash, but *none* for recovering an ammo card that gets detached like this.&nbsp; It would make more sense to me to errata the ammo cards to say \"If the Gun card this is next to is destroyed, so is this card.\"Oh, wait.&nbsp; Is there actually a situation that can destroy a gun instead of an ammo card that isn't due to players being perverse and/or dumb about their choice of resolving ambiguities?&nbsp; I don't think there is.&nbsp; Nevermind...&nbsp;\n<p>Why = because Christopher said so.</p>\n<blockquote></blockquote>\n<blockquote>For the second instance of damage, Dual Crowbars is only restricted from selecting the target that was the final result of the first instance of damage (after all redirects).\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote>\nIs this a general change to how targeting multiple targets works? I was under the impression that you couldn't re-choose the *initial* target, but you *could* re-choose a target that ended up getting damaged earlier due to redirects.&nbsp; Is this still true in general, or not?&nbsp;\n<p>No, it's not a change, just a clarification of Dual Crowbars in case anyone didn't know. Most of the things in the post are re-iterating existing clarifications, not changes. They are answers to questions we asked Christopher.</p>",
